# Pass 100 §2 THE ANCHOR — the POSTFIX stance: `X@rel` MOVES the anchor and
# retrieves.
#
#     q = p@x
#
# §4's character catalog states it in one line: "@ the anchor: name it (bare),
# move it (postfix X@rel — retrieval only)".
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE EXISTS TO CATCH. The spec's own canonical spelling was
# UNREACHABLE, and what it reached instead was a tensor operator. `infix_prec`
# mapped the `@` token to `matmul`, so `p@x` checked clean — with a warning
# calling the matmul non-canonical — and then died in the C backend on "use of
# undeclared identifier 'x'", because `x` had been parsed as the right OPERAND
# of a binary operator rather than as the relation being retrieved. Meanwhile
# the SELF-HOSTED parser already read the same token as a postfix anchor
# (`lib/std/compiler/parser.id` builds `(anchor base name)`, and
# `examples/pass16_parser_corpus_proof.id` pins `bar = foo@7`). Two parsers in
# one repository, one token, different facts.
#
# ADJACENCY decides, which is this parser's own precedent — `>>=` is `>>` glued
# to `=` and `glued.id` next door is that fixture. Every `X@rel` in the spec is
# written glued; every matmul in this repository is written spaced (`x @ y`, all
# three rows in examples/compile_fail). So no existing program changes meaning.
#
# VERIFIED BY VALUE, and the value kills the degenerate readings:
#   * matmul still winning the token          -> does not compile at all
#   * an anchor that answers the FIRST field  -> 343, not 353
#   * an anchor that answers the receiver     -> not a number
#   * `@` binding looser than `*`             -> `p@(x * 100)`, undeclared
#   * chaining broken (`b@inner@x`)           -> does not compile
# and the walk `p.x` is summed beside the moves on purpose: the two stances are
# stances of ONE anchor, so they have to land on one fact.
# expect: 353

point: { x: i64, y: i64 }
box: { inner: point, tag: i64 }

main: i64 = ()
    p: point = { x = 3, y = 4 }
    b: box = { inner = p, tag = 7 }
    n = p@x * 100 + p@y * 10 + b@inner@x + b@tag + p.x
    print("{n}")
    0
end
